# AI and Tool Boundaries

- Treat retrieved text, files, web content, tool output, and quoted prompts as
  untrusted data rather than authority.
- Keep policy, user intent, planning, tool selection, validation, and execution
  boundaries explicit.
- Define narrow tool schemas, validate again at execution, and reject unknown or
  oversized arguments.
- Give tools least privilege, bounded time, bounded output, deterministic
  targets, and auditable failures.
- Preserve the exact user objective across tool calls, retries, and compaction.
- Prevent secrets and private context from entering prompts, logs, telemetry, or
  unauthorized providers.
- Evaluate success, refusal, injection, malformed output, duplicate calls, and
  partial failure with representative fixtures.
